\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Flat determinant and nonzero scalar curvature}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Let the two periods be even and at least #{4}, let #{a=1}, and take a fiber of dimension #{2}. Give the first direction the identity link and the second direction the scalar #{(-1)^{x_1}} times the identity. Every scalar plaquette is #{-1} while its determinant is #{1}. The [plaquette angle](fdip-015V) on the represented determinant line vanishes everywhere. On the scalar line the plaquettes reach the cut.}

\p{On site fields depending only on the parity of #{x_1}, the first shift exchanges the even and odd values and the second acts by the respective signs #{1,-1}. Denote these restrictions by #{X,Z}. They are self-adjoint involutions with #{XZ=-ZX}, so the centered terms vanish there. The unshifted Wilson term is #{2\bbI -X-Z}. The operator #{X+Z} is traceless on the two parity values and has square #{2\bbI}, hence has an eigenvalue #{\sqrt2}. At #{M=2-\sqrt2} the Wilson kernel is singular. Thus vanishing determinant angles alone cannot ensure the regularity used in \ref{fdip-019X}; the angle there is measured on the scalar line.}
